    "body": "## Summary\n\nSince tablib 3.10.0, `tablib/__init__.py` declares `__lazy_modules__ = {\"tablib.core\"}` (#645). On Python 3.15 this means `import tablib` no longer executes `tablib/core.py`, and therefore never runs `registry.register_builtins()` (the call at the bottom of `core.py`).\n\nAs a result, `tablib.formats.registry` is empty after `import tablib` until some code happens to touch `tablib.core` (for example by referencing `tablib.Dataset`). Any consumer that resolves formats through the registry directly gets `UnsupportedFormat` for every builtin format, including `csv` and `json`.\n\nOn Python 3.14 and earlier the `__lazy_modules__` declaration is ignored, so the registry is populated eagerly as before.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```python\nimport tablib\nfrom tablib.formats import registry\n\nprint(list(registry._formats))\nregistry.get_format(\"csv\")\n```\n\nPython 3.14.3, tablib 3.10.0:\n\n```\n['json', 'csv', 'tsv', 'dbf', 'html', 'jira', 'latex', 'rst', 'sql']\n```\n\nPython 3.15.0, tablib 3.10.0:\n\n```\n[]\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  ...\ntablib.exceptions.UnsupportedFormat: Tablib has no format 'csv' or it is not registered.\n```\n\nTouching `tablib.core` first (e.g. `import tablib.core`) populates the registry and the lookup succeeds.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe registry has been populated as a side effect of `import tablib` since the registry was introduced, and `Dataset` relies on the same mechanism. Downstream libraries that call `registry.get_format()` or iterate `registry.formats()` without first instantiating a `Dataset` now see an order-dependent failure that only appears on the newest Python.\n\nA concrete example: django-import-export checks format availability via `registry.get_format()` at startup, and on 3.15 it silently dropped CSV from its default formats because CSV was the first format checked against the empty registry (django-import-export/django-import-export#2210, fixed downstream in django-import-export/django-import-export#2211 by importing `tablib.core` eagerly).\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nEither of these keeps the startup-time benefit for everything except the registration step:\n\n1. Remove `\"tablib.core\"` from `__lazy_modules__` in `tablib/__init__.py`, so `core` (and hence `register_builtins()`) is imported eagerly as before. The expensive optional format modules are already loaded lazily by the registry's string-path descriptors, so this should cost little.\n2. Move the `registry.register_builtins()` call out of `core.py` into `tablib/__init__.py` (or into `tablib/formats/__init__.py`) so registration does not depend on `core` being imported.\n\nHappy to send a PR for whichever approach you prefer.\n\n## Environment\n\n- tablib 3.10.0\n- Python 3.15.0 (fails) and 3.14.3 (works)\n- macOS, but the behaviour is import-order based and should be platform independent\n",
