    "body": "I noticed the DBF code does the same work over and over.\n\nOn import, it reads the whole row again for each field. So a row with 100 fields gets read and decoded 100 times, even though we only need to read it once. It also searches the field names for every value.\n\nOn export, it searches the field names again for every cell, even though their positions stay the same.\n\nCould we find the positions once and reuse them? For import, we could also read each row once and take all its values from that row.\n\nThe repeated cell work is roughly O(rows × columns²). This could bring that part down to expected O(columns + rows × columns), using O(columns) extra space for the positions. That only describes the repeated cell work; there is still other work, like building the DBF header or writing the file.\n\nDuplicate names, names with different letter cases, bad or empty input, and custom streams/datasets should still work the same as before. Small inputs can keep the old code if the new setup does not help.\n\nThe code is in [src/tablib/formats/_dbf.py](https://github.com/jazzband/tablib/blob/966876c76a6378557c1951badc1665b4d1375875/src/tablib/formats/_dbf.py), in DBFFormat.import_set and DBFFormat.export_set.\n",
